<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Neapolis</h1><p class="st-meta">470 BCE – 326 BCE (approx.) · capital Neapolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek 'new city' on the Bay of Naples, founded from Cumae, which kept its Greek speech and customs long after becoming Rome's ally in 326 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a> founded Neapolis around 470 BCE beside an older settlement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenope-city">Parthenope</a>, named after a Siren whose tomb was shown there and which became known as Palaepolis, 'old city'. The new town was laid out on a regular grid whose main streets still shape central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Neapolis grew through trade and gained importance after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cumae">Cumae</a> fell to the Campanians around 421 BCE. Athenian settlers are said to have joined it in the 5th century BCE, and it later admitted Campanian families to citizenship. Its coins with a man-headed bull and its torch race in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenope-city">Parthenope</a> are well known. It remained a centre of Greek culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a Roman siege in 327–326 BCE, the city made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on favourable terms and became a loyal naval ally. It kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and institutions into the Empire, attracting Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>, and grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 470 BCE – 326 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:50.35%;width:2.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>470 BCE – 326 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neapolis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Naples (ancient), Parthenope</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-398&amp;focus=neapolis" aria-label="Neapolis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5063 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Neapolis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5383 1098L5344 975M5383 1098L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5344 975h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Neapolis. <a href="/atlas?year=-398&amp;focus=neapolis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
